[claude] 🔧 mcp__kernelgen__finalize_round: {"conclusion": {"round_num": 1, "expectation_status": "baseline", "root_cause": "All 24 workloads are numerically correct (max abs err 1.95e-3 on bf16, 3.9e-6 on fp32) but the kernel costs ~37.8 ns per output element, exactly linear in element count and identical for fp16/bf16/fp32 -> the cost is per-lane addressing, not bandwidth. The four tl.load calls use vector-of-pointers addresses derived from div/mod chains, so they lower to per-lane non-affine loads instead of block transfers. A target probe showed contiguous block load+store of 8.4M elements takes ~0.03 ms net while the same element count with 4 non-affine loads takes 9.9 ms, i.e. a ~300x penalty for the gather path.", "perf_gap_analysis": "Baseline: geo_mean 0.00163, worst workload timing-1 (0.00071x, 290 ms vs 0.207 ms reference). Device probe evidence: (1) contiguous copy of the timing-1 volume is ~0.03 ms net, so memory is not the limit; (2) per-program overhead on this part is ~37 ns, so program counts in the tens of thousands are themselves expensive; (3) a 2-D tile whose row base is a data-dependent vector but whose last dimension is contiguous (ptr + h0[:,None]*IW + arange(IW)) runs 4.2M elements in 0.188 ms, i.e. the backend handles row-gather with a contiguous minor dimension well; (4) UB register gathers measured at least 9x cheaper than global gather. Conclusion: replace per-lane global gathers with (a) contiguous minor-dimension loads and (b) either an affine expansion for exact integer upscale factors or a UB gather.", "next_suggestion": "Rewrite as a row-tiled kernel: BR output rows per program, contiguous (BR, IW) input row loads with a data-dependent row base, vertical combine, then horizontal resample with no global gather. For the general case use tl.gather on the register tile; for exact integer horizontal upscale factors (OW = 2*IW, as in every timing workload) use a fully affine expansion: load the row shifted by -1/0/+1, build the two interleaved weight branches and select on a length-S constexpr axis so the store stays contiguous. Keep the current flat gather kernel as the correct general fallback.", "optimization_level": "L1_architecture", "debug_lesson": "A modulo-based 'gather' probe is only a real gather if the period does not divide the block size: with BLK=1024 and IWP=4096 the index stayed contiguous inside each block, so the first probe badly under-estimated the gather cost and had to be discarded. Always check that a synthetic irregular-index probe actually destroys affine structure.", "strategy_evolution": "Round 1 targeted correctness first (all 24 pass, including the 1x1 outputs that previously failed to compile) and used the measurement to falsify 'bandwidth-bound' in favour of 'per-lane addressing bound'.", "knowledge_assessments": []}}
